    <a href="#manifesto-related">Related resources</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="b73-assembly" aria-labelledby="b73-assembly-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="b73-assembly-title">The B73 reference assembly</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p>It is imperative that the community work from the same genome coordinate system across projects, so that data generated by different groups can be fully leveraged and displayed comparably. Like many model organism databases, MaizeGDB is charged with facilitating that, and is committed to releasing official genome assemblies as they become available.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="manifesto-timeline">
      <li>
        <div class="manifesto-when">November 2009</div>
        <div class="manifesto-what">
          <h3>B73 RefGen_v1</h3>
          <p>The first complete assembly of B73 as the representative maize genome, published in <cite>Science</cite>&#58; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1178534">The B73 maize genome&#58; complexity, diversity, and dynamics</a>, Schnable et al. 2009.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="manifesto-when">November 2010</div>
        <div class="manifesto-what">
          <h3>B73 RefGen_v2</h3>
          <p>Calculated by the Maize Genome Sequencing Consortium and made the default view of the assembly at MaizeGDB. It reached GenBank on 7 December 2012 under BioProject <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/bioproject/10769">10769</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="manifesto-when">April 2013</div>
        <div class="manifesto-what">
          <h3>B73 RefGen_v3</h3>
          <p>Not a global re-assembly. Roche/454 reads from a whole-genome shotgun library were assembled into contigs with ABySS and aligned to RefGen_v2 to capture gene space missing within and between the original BACs. About 65,000 full-length cDNAs from the <a href="http://www.maizecdna.org">Maize Full-Length cDNA project</a> &#40;<a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1000740">Soderlund et al. 2009</a>&#41; were aligned to both the v2 contigs and the new ones. This was the consortium's final product.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="manifesto-when">August 2016</div>
        <div class="manifesto-what">
          <h3><a href="/genome/assembly/Zm-B73-REFERENCE-GRAMENE-4.0">Zm-B73-REFERENCE-GRAMENE-4.0</a></h3>
          <p>An entirely new assembly &#40;B73 RefGen_v4&#41;, built from PacBio single-molecule real-time sequencing at roughly 60-fold coverage and scaffolded with high-resolution whole-genome optical mapping &mdash; without the BAC physical map that had guided v1 to v3. The pseudomolecules were assembled nearly end to end, a 52-fold improvement in average contig size over v3. Funded by <a href="https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=1127112">NSF award 1127112</a> to <a href="https://www.gramene.org">Gramene</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="manifesto-current">
        <div class="manifesto-when">January 2020</div>
        <div class="manifesto-what">
          <h3><a href="/genome/assembly/Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0">Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0</a> <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-ok">Current reference</span></h3>
          <p>Version 5.0 of the B73 genome &#40;<a href="/data_center/stock?id=47638">PI 677128</a>&#41;, released by the NAM Consortium alongside the 25 NAM founders. Produced with PacBio long reads and a mate-pair strategy; scaffolds validated by BioNano optical mapping and ordered and oriented using linkage and pan-genome marker data.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="nam-founders" aria-labelledby="nam-founders-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="nam-founders-title">The NAM founder assemblies</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p><b>January 2020.</b> Genome assemblies of the 25 NAM founders are a valuable addition to maize genomic resources, spanning a broad cross-section of maize genetic variation. They complement the B73 representative genome by adding pan-genome sequence that B73 does not carry, and give the community both a view of genomic variation across maize and a pan-genome to work from.</p>
      <p>They were produced by the <a href="https://nam-genomes.github.io/">NAM Sequencing Consortium</a> using PacBio long reads and a mate-pair strategy, with scaffolds validated by BioNano optical mapping and ordered and oriented using linkage and pan-genome marker data.</p>
      <p class="manifesto-cite"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abg5289">De novo assembly, annotation, and comparative analysis of 26 diverse maize genomes</a>. Hufford et al. 2021, <cite>Science</cite>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="more-assemblies" aria-labelledby="more-assemblies-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="more-assemblies-title">Additional genome assemblies</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p>For many years the B73 genome was the only reference-quality maize assembly available, because sequencing and assembling a genome of about 2.1 Gb was expensive. B73 remains the representative assembly, but many more high-quality assemblies now exist, and their number is expected to grow as sequencing costs fall and assembly methods improve. Increasingly they are completed in sets &#40;the NAM founders, for instance&#41; and can be used both within their set and against every other available assembly.</p>
      <p><b>Nothing is withdrawn without being asked for.</b> Unless a group requests that a version be withdrawn, MaizeGDB maintains all past assembly data even when improved versions are produced. Detailed information on every assembly MaizeGDB hosts is in the <a href="/genome">Genome Data Hub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="pan-genes" aria-labelledby="pan-genes-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="pan-genes-title">Pan-gene analyses</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p>The 25 NAM founder assemblies and annotations, together with version 5.0 of the B73 genome, made comprehensive pan-genomes possible. The first set of maize pan-genes was calculated by MaizeGDB staff over 58 annotations using the <a href="https://github.com/legumeinfo/pandagma">Pandagma</a> pipeline, and presented in a new pan-gene data hub in November 2023.</p>
      <p>The set has grown since. Current pan-gene counts, the annotations behind them and the gene families they group are in the <a href="/pan_gene_center/pan_gene">Pan-Gene Data Hub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="nomenclature" aria-labelledby="nomenclature-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="nomenclature-title">Assembly and gene model nomenclature</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p>A well-developed nomenclature is necessary to prevent confusion and to carry as much information as possible without becoming cumbersome. It has to account for species-specific information, so that the exact inbred line used and the project metadata can be reached easily.</p>
      <p>The move from GRMZM identifiers to the current nomenclature was necessary for several reasons, the main one being to say which maize line a gene model came from. That matters particularly in maize, which is well documented to carry substantial presence/absence variation &#40;PAV&#41; and copy number variation &#40;CNV&#41; across inbred lines. To make the transition easier, the older nomenclature is kept as a synonym and can still be used to look up gene models at MaizeGDB.</p>
      <p>The rules in force are on the <a href="/nomenclature">nomenclature page</a> and in the <a href="https://documents.maizegdb.org/nomenclature/maize_assembly_nomenclature_2021.pdf">2021 assembly nomenclature standard</a>. The <a href="https://documents.maizegdb.org/nomenclature/maize_assembly_nomenclature_2016_update.pdf">2016 update</a> that preceded it is kept for reference.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="functional-annotation" aria-labelledby="functional-annotation-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="functional-annotation-title">MaizeGDB accepts functional annotation</h2></div>
    </div>

    <div class="manifesto-prose">
      <p>Functional annotation means different things to different people. Broadly it is the attaching of information to a genomic DNA sequence&#58; gene product identity, biological or biochemical function, expression, regulation, interactions.</p>
      <p>Are you generating RNA-seq data you would like aligned to assemblies, to show that the genes in a region are expressed? Do you have a mutation in a gene that is mapped to an assembly, with a known mutant phenotype? Have you determined the temporal and spatial regulation of a small group of transcription factors? MaizeGDB is interested in functional annotation sets both small and large, from <i>in silico</i> analysis or from experimental validation. <a href="/contact">Contact us</a> to find out how yours can be included.</p>
      <p>MaizeGDB also accepts functional annotation based on assigning <a href="https://geneontology.org">Gene Ontology</a> terms to gene structures. A GO term assigned to a gene requires a standard evidence code documenting how the inference of function was made&#58; an annotation from a published, peer-reviewed experiment carries EXP, while one from an enzyme assay carries IDA. The full list is in the Gene Ontology Consortium's <a href="https://geneontology.org/docs/guide-go-evidence-codes/">guide to evidence codes</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="guidelines" aria-labelledby="guidelines-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="guidelines-title">Guidelines for groups planning a maize genome</h2></div>
    </div>

    <p class="manifesto-lead">Suggested elements of a successful collaboration with the maize community, for groups intending to sequence, assemble and annotate a maize genome for submission to MaizeGDB.</p>

    <ol class="manifesto-guidelines">
      <li>
        <h3>A plan for documentation that is complete, accurate and timely</h3>
        <p>A centrally accessible plan should be available when the project begins, including a timeline for data delivery. Functional and structural annotation should carry standard evidence codes, clearly separating annotation with experimental evidence from purely <i>in silico</i> analysis.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>A plan for working closely with MaizeGDB as the ultimate disseminator</h3>
        <p>Assemblies and annotations should reach MaizeGDB regularly and promptly. MaizeGDB can publish the delivery dates to keep the community informed; ideally those dates are known in advance and kept to. MaizeGDB will create a genome assembly page displaying your project metadata. Delays happen &mdash; the point is to make the process transparent to the research community.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>A way to talk to the maize community directly and with one voice</h3>
        <p>Maize researchers are a vibrant community, at every level and in both the public and private sectors. A two-way channel should be in place from the start of the project, so the community can take in new information and respond to it quickly. The goal is that everyone gets the same information at the same time and can plan accordingly. FAQs, blogs, social media and conferences can all serve; consider all of them, to reach as many stakeholders as possible.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>A robust way to capture annotation from the community</h3>
        <p>For any assembly, researchers hold high-quality structural and functional annotation for their genes of interest, on lab computers and in publications. They are usually willing to share it, but there is currently no robust way to collect it. Groups developing assemblies are encouraged to work with MaizeGDB on a plan for gathering high-value annotations specific to theirs. Everything submitted this way is vetted by MaizeGDB curators before it enters the assembly, with an indication of who provided it. The volume arriving by this route will be comparatively small, but the quality is expected to be very high.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="manifesto-note">For the practical steps &mdash; identifiers, the metadata template, the INSDC requirement and the hosting levels &mdash; see <a href="/contribute_data#genomic">submitting genome assemblies to MaizeGDB</a>.</p>
  </section>

